# Wire Rail II: how a payment gets from the intake API to the OPF submission

Options evaluated, and the one we propose.

## 1. The decision in one paragraph

The flow is agreed: the Wire Rail II API accepts a payment and returns, a separate submission service sends it to the OPF API, and a callback tells Payment Exchange the outcome. What is not agreed is the mechanism between "accepted" and "submitted": what holds the payment, what wakes the submission service, and what retries when OPF is slow or down.

**We propose that the DynamoDB table is the queue.** The record the API writes is the unit of work. It carries its own status, its attempt count and the time of its next attempt. The submission service finds due records through a small index, claims one with a conditional write, submits it, and writes the result back. No separate queue sits between the two services. DynamoDB Streams or a queue can be added later as a faster wake-up, and nothing in the state logic changes when they are.

The short reason: every option needs this table and this state logic anyway, to know the status of each wire and to survive the loss of a region. A queue in front of it removes none of that work and adds a second place where the state of a payment lives.

The submission service is not interim code. It is the part of the rail that stays when OPF goes, and later channels will reuse it. So the design is judged on whether it lasts: a new intake, a new channel and more load must each be an addition, not a redesign.

## 2. What the mechanism has to do

These are the requirements the reviews raised, plus the ones that follow from wires being wires and from the rail being permanent.

| # | Requirement | Why it matters here |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | Nothing accepted is ever lost | The API has told Payment Exchange "accepted". From then on the rail owns the wire. |
| R2 | Survive an OPF outage of several hours | OPF is an external API. Payments must wait and then drain on their own. |
| R3 | Back off, and do not hammer a vendor that is down | Exponential back-off per payment, and one probe instead of a retry storm. |
| R4 | Survive the loss of a region | Payments waiting in the failed region must be picked up in the other one. |
| R5 | Never create a duplicate wire | Retries, replays from Payment Exchange and two regions must not send the same wire twice. |
| R6 | Always know where each wire is | Operations must be able to list what is waiting, why, and since when, and replay one. |
| R7 | Stop waiting at some point | A wire has a value date and a cut-off. Past a deadline it must be escalated, not retried forever. |
| R8 | Stay independent of the channel | A later phase adds correspondent banks, Fed or SWIFT without changing the intake. |
| R9 | Stay independent of the intake | Today the intake is an API call. Later it may be an event stream. The work after "accepted" must not care. |
| R10 | Grow without a redesign | More wires, more workers and more channels are handled by adding capacity or configuration. |

What weighs most for a wire is durability, recoverability and visibility. A wire that is late is a problem. A wire that is lost or sent twice is an incident. The options are judged on those first, and on growth second.

Two existing safety nets also matter:
- **The FIS idempotency key.** Every call to OPF carries a key that must be the same on a retry. We use the UETR. A repeated call with the same key is treated by FIS as a retry, not as a new wire.
- **The Payment Exchange replay.** If a stage does not complete within its service level, the Arbiter replays the previous stage with the same UETR, across regions.

## 3. The options

### Option A: SQS queue with a dead-letter queue

The API writes the record, then puts a message on an SQS queue. The submission service polls the queue. A failed message becomes visible again after a delay and is retried. After a set number of receives it moves to a dead-letter queue.

Good:
- Managed, well known, and the pattern most teams reach for. Alarms on queue depth and on the dead-letter queue come almost for free.
- Scales consumers easily, with queue depth as the scaling signal.

Problems for this use:
- **A queue lives in one region.** SQS does not replicate. If a region fails, the messages in its queue are unreachable until it returns. To meet R4 we would need a sweeper in the other region that finds accepted-but-not-submitted records in the table and submits them. When the failed region returns, its queue still holds those messages and its consumers process them again.
- **Two writes that cannot be made atomic.** The record goes to DynamoDB and the message goes to SQS. If the second write fails, the payment is stored and never queued. The same sweeper is the fix.
- **Back-off is bounded.** A message can stay hidden for at most 12 hours per receive, and can be delayed at most 15 minutes on send. Back-off is done by changing the visibility timeout on each failure. It works, but every failed attempt counts toward the dead-letter threshold, so a long outage either fills the dead-letter queue or forces a very high threshold.
- **Two sources of truth.** The message says "to do", the record says the status. They can disagree, and the consumer must always check the record before acting.
- **A queue per channel.** Each new channel needs its own queue, dead-letter queue and routing.

Verdict: workable, and our second choice. But once the sweeper and the status check exist, the queue is carrying nothing that the table does not already carry.

### Option B: DynamoDB Streams as the trigger, on its own

The API writes the record. DynamoDB emits a change event. A consumer, in practice a Lambda function, is invoked with the event and submits the payment.

Good:
- One write. No message to lose between the table and a queue.
- Very fast on the normal path.

Problems for this use:
- **A stream is a trigger, not a retry scheduler.** A stream keeps its records for 24 hours. A failing batch is retried straight away, again and again, with no per-record delay. There is no "try this one again in 15 minutes".
- **One bad record blocks the ones behind it** in the same shard, unless batches are split and failures are routed away. The failure destination receives a pointer to the record, not the record, so it must be read back within the same 24 hours.
- **With a table replicated across regions, every region sees every change** in its own stream. Without a claim on the record, both regions would submit.
- The natural consumer is Lambda. That is a new kind of compute for this rail, with its own proxy egress, secrets and detokenisation access. The Fargate service we are building anyway cannot consume a stream without extra client machinery.

Verdict: good as a wake-up signal, not sufficient as the mechanism. It does not meet R2, R3 or R7 on its own.

### Option C: the table is the queue (proposed)

The record is the unit of work. The submission service polls a small index of due records, claims one with a conditional write, submits it and writes the outcome back. Section 5 describes it in detail.

Good:
- **One write, one source of truth.** Accepted means stored. Stored means it will be worked. There is no gap between the two.
- **Back-off without a ceiling.** The next attempt time is a field. It can be seconds or hours away.
- **Region failover is the same mechanism.** With the table replicated to both regions, the waiting payments are already in the surviving region. Its workers pick them up.
- **Visibility is a query.** "What is waiting, since when, after how many attempts, with which last error" is a read of the index.
- **The intake can change.** An API call or a stream consumer both end in the same conditional write. Nothing after it changes.
- **A new channel is a value, not new infrastructure.** A second submission service reads the same table under its own queue name, with its own breaker.
- **No new infrastructure to start.** The table exists. The worker runs inside the Fargate submission service.

Problems:
- **We own the scheduling code**: the claim, the lease, the back-off and the circuit breaker. It is a small state machine, but it is ours to get right, to test and to maintain for as long as the rail lives.
- **Polling has known growing pains.** With many workers, several try to claim the same record and all but one fail. The normal path waits for the next poll. One channel's backlog can crowd the others. Each has a standard answer, set out in section 5, and each answer is more code we own.

Verdict: meets every requirement with the fewest parts, and grows by configuration.

### Option D: Step Functions, one workflow per payment

The API starts a workflow for each payment. The workflow calls the submission step, with retry and back-off declared in the workflow definition, and can wait for up to a year.

Good: back-off, waiting and a visual history per payment are built in. Failed runs can be redriven.

Problems: a workflow lives in one region, so R4 has the same answer as SQS, a sweeper on the table. Starting the workflow is a second write after the record. A circuit breaker across all payments does not fit a per-payment workflow. It is a new platform for the team and must be an approved pattern.

Verdict: elegant per payment, weak on region loss and on a vendor-wide outage.

### Option E: a Kafka topic

The API publishes to a topic and the submission service consumes it. Payment Exchange is itself built on an event stream, so the tooling exists.

Problems: a failed record blocks its partition unless retry topics are built, one per delay tier. Hours of back-off mean several retry topics and consumers. Publishing after the record is again a second write. A topic is a good way for payments to arrive at the rail. It is a poor place to hold a payment that must wait for a vendor.

Verdict: built for ordered, continuous flow. Waiting, back-off and a per-payment deadline work against its model.

### Option F: stay synchronous and let Payment Exchange replay

This is the design we are leaving. It is listed for completeness. The replay settings cover about an hour, the caller holds a connection open on an API whose latency we do not know, and a long outage turns into snagged payments that people must replay by hand. The architecture review has already ruled it out.

